@0    = ~You! You are the ones I need to talk to.~
@1    = ~And you came!~
@2    = ~Please, please listen to me!~
@3    = ~You found the dead monk. I'm not a monster!~
@4    = ~I guess they told you I'm a reckless thief.~
@5    = ~I need to explain what happened. And... I need your help.~
@6    = ~Fine, I'll listen.~
@7    = ~I'm not helping you get out of prison!~
@8    = ~Why did you request for me?~
@9    = ~Why would I listen to you?~
@10   = ~What? No! This is not what this is about at *all*!~
@11   = ~Why me?~
@12   = ~I... I will give you gold. I took some before I came here, right? I still have some of it and will tell you where I put it.~
@13   = ~You are the ones who cleared the Nashkel mines. There was discussion between the commander and the Flaming Fist about whether you might be a threat or a help, and they settled on "help" for now in the hope that you are not beholden to Amn. I need a mercenary who can fight and is ready to face a threat others might run from. You have proven to be such a <PRO_MANWOMAN>.~
@14   = ~It's about my brother. He is in trouble... He is the reason I took the gold.~
@15   = ~It's the gold I wanted... I never meant to hurt Martina. The poor woman... I know she and Lenard were lovers. I am sorry she died! It was an accident, I swear. I needed the gold to help my brother!~
@16   = ~My brother lives in Nashkel and is being threatened by smugglers. He had... a bad time and lost himself to black lotus for a while. But that is long over! He has... obligations he hasn't been able to satisfy, and now they've taken my gold and still threaten him. Please, help us!~
@17   = ~So, your brother is addicted to black lotus and you expect me to bail him out of his debts?~
@18   = ~Smugglers in Nashkel? Tell me more.~
@19   = ~Smugglers in Nashkel? Hm... I might have an idea who that would be. Tell me more.~
@20   = ~And you expect me to do what?~
@21   = ~I paid all of his debts! But they said it's too late and that he will be sold if I do not bring more. I came back here to get help, but my... deeds were already known, so now I'm stuck here in prison.~
@22   = ~Someone needs to confront those bastards. Please, you have proven you are capable of fighting. I will make this worthwhile for you!~
@23   = ~There was supposed to be a second meeting where I was to bring more gold, at the black lotus tent in Nashkel. I came here to ask for some days off, but I was arrested... Please, take the gold and bring it to the smugglers. Or keep the gold and confront them—they are no match for you, I swear!~
@24   = ~Why didn't you tell this to your commander?~
@25   = ~The gold belongs to the monks of Ilmater! I cannot take it to smugglers, I am sorry.~
@26   = ~I will do so, but not with the gold you stole. Tell me where it is so I can return what is left!~
@27   = ~Fine, tell me where the gold is.~
@28   = ~You expect me to confront a gang of smugglers *after* I get the gold? You are a bit naive, don't you think?~
@29   = ~This is not only a personal problem! It's about smugglers who threaten decent townsfolk, and... there will be enough riches involved to pay everyone! Please help my brother!~
@30   = ~Nope, not interested.~
@31   = ~I need someone who is capable of going to Nashkel... I could ask the captain for help but what can he or the Flaming Fist do? Nothing! They are of no help there, in Amnian lands! Also... they would return the gold to the monastery and then all hope would be lost...~
@32   = ~I'm not going to do that, sorry.~
@33   = ~I couldn't care less about your brother, fool.~
@34   = ~That means you will help my brother?~
@35   = ~Yes, I will help him. Tell me what to do.~
@36   = ~I didn't say that.~
@37   = ~I have no choice... Please, it is more gold than they demanded. You get paid for your services. They will not attack you if you come as a messenger, I swear! They are just a bunch of thieves smuggling wares to save the harbor fees. They are not bloodthirsty if you don't attack them!~
@38   = ~You just said they enslaved your brother! Sounds like dangerous folks to me.~
@39   = ~You seem to know them quite well, you know.~
@40   = ~No, it's a bit more complicated than that... The smugglers threatened my brother's safety, but only if I do not deliver the gold in time!~
@41   = ~*sigh* My brother was in trouble a *lot*. It's not the first time I had to pay him off, but this time it was by far the greatest sum. Please!~
@42   = ~Please! This is the last hope for my brother!~
@43   = ~I am sorry, your brother got himself into this situation. I do not have time to deal with smugglers currently.~
@44   = ~You... you wouldn't...?~
@45   = ~*sigh* Fine, I'll do it. Tell me what I need to know.~
@46   = ~I get it, you are not the helping type. But as I said, there is more gold to be had... especially if you would consider working with the smugglers. They are always on the lookout for helpers... I know, because... they offered me gold a thousand times if I used my connections for them.~
@47   = ~Hm. Now *that* sounds interesting. Fine, tell me more. I'll see what I can do for your brother along the way.~
@48   = ~*moans* Then all hope is lost. They will find a way to make him pay, and I'm in prison. Leave me be, "hero of the Nashkel mines".~
@49   = ~Thank you! I thank you! First, you take the gold out of the trunk. It's in the Dragon Tower, that's the one right by the armorer, top floor. You need to open it all the way to see the hidden compartment, or the gold can easily be missed.~
@50   = ~Wait, the gold is inside your trunk here in the tower? Are you kidding me?!~
@51   = ~Very well, then what?~
@52   = ~Not... not mine. It's one of the sergeant's, but he is on his week off.~
@53   = ~Ah, smart. And where do I meet the smugglers?~
@54   = ~Do you know where the black lotus tent in Nashkel is? It's to the far east of the carnival. From the tent, just go a little off to the south until the tents can't be seen anymore, between the trees. That's where the smugglers meet, usually at dusk, but there is always a contact there, if you know where to look.~
@55   = ~Give them 1,000 gold and tell them it's from me. They should let my brother go then. Whatever you choose to do—paying or fighting or working with the smugglers—please see to my brother being freed.~
@56   = ~I thank you, <CHARNAME>. And—please hurry! There is not much time left.~
@57   = ~Did you have a chance to speak to my brother? Is he in the monastery?~
@58   = ~Indeed. He is safe and sound in the Ilmater's Monastery, recovering from his imprisonment.~
@59   = ~Nothing new yet.~
@60   = ~There you are again! Any news from my brother?~
@61   = ~The sum is paid. He will be safe shortly.~
@62   = ~I paid 20,000 gold to the slave traders. He will be safe shortly.~
@63   = ~Well, apparently he was sold to slave traders... I'm on it, though.~
@64   = ~I know how to buy him free, but the slavers want 20,000 gold!~
@65   = ~Well... I think I was a bit too ambitious there. The smugglers didn't have time to tell me where your brother is now, but at least I killed the lot!~
@66   = ~Hm, I decided I don't care. Sorry.~
@67   = ~I waited too long to make the deal with the slave traders... I am sorry, he is out of my reach.~
@68   = ~Well... I confronted the slave traders too early, so I don't know where he is. I am sorry.~
@69   = ~I thank you for your help. Please hurry! I hope it's not too late...~
@70   = ~All hope is lost... all hope...~
@71   = ~Sl... slave traders?! I never expected them to go that far!~
@72   = ~We'll... we'll talk later. Please leave...~
@73   = ~That... that much? I don't have anything... I have nothing left... I can't expect you to raise such a sum. He is lost then...~
@74   = ~Not yet. I will see what I can do.~
@75   = ~I am sorry, but there is no way I will spend such a sum for someone I don't even know.~
@76   = ~Yes, he is.~
@77   = ~*sigh* I thank you for going this far, "hero of the Nashkel mines"!~
@78   = ~He... he is?~
@79   = ~You paid 20,000 gold to free someone you don't even know! I thank you! Thank you!~
@80   = ~We are in your debt! Nathoriel is... not the best person, but it is not only his fault. Father died when we were young, and mother fell to black lotus... I raised him, and... I failed to make him a good person. But ah, what am I lamenting. I'm in prison, and what deeds have I done!~
@81   = ~I thank you for your help. I will pray for you every day.~
@82   = ~Something tells me it was more complicated than just giving the smugglers the gold I had. I thank you! Thank you!~
@83   = ~You are the ones who saved me from those blasted slave traders! I don't know how to repay you. No, I mean it—I don't know how I'll ever repay the sum you spent to free me, so please show a merciful heart and just let it drop.~
@84   = ~I appreciate the effort you took to free me, I really do.~
@85   = ~That's also a way to say "thank you", I guess.~
@86   = ~You are lucky I was ready to part with such a great sum for someone I don't even know.~
@87   = ~The Seatower guard paid for you, actually. The commander will have a question or two for you regarding the slavers.~
@88   = ~Now what? I thought I'd put you in my mine where you could work off your debts for the rest of your life.~
@89   = ~Regain your strength now. The monks should see to your recovery.~
@90   = ~Well then, goodbye.~
@91   = ~What? You are kidding, I hope? I should be safe here in the monastery, and slavery is against the law in Baldur's Gate!~
@92   = ~I was kidding—I don't have a mine.~
@93   = ~You are here for one reason only: so the commander gets the needed information on *where* the slavers operate!~
@94   = ~Ah, er, haha...? You are kidding though, right? I really do not know how to raise this much gold, even if I tried to.~
@95   = ~You have my sincerest gratitude, but that's about all I can give you.~
@96   = ~Oh, but we could get to know each other. How about you invite me to a mug of nice dragon ale over in the inn, for example.~
@97   = ~I'm starting to regret the trouble I went through to save you.~
@98   = ~Have a good day then. And, say 'hello' to my brother, will you? The shiny knight stuck in prison, who would have guessed.~
@99   = ~Yes, that's what Shuck keeps saying, but there he is, getting himself into prison to save his lost little brother.~
@100  = ~You disgust me, to be honest.~
@101  = ~Well, ain't I lucky you freed me before realizing this.~
@102  = ~*wails* You leave me at the commander's mercy? Didn't you hear about his interrogator? I'm just a weakened victim of the slave trade. It's not fair to throw me into the law's mouth like this!~
@103  = ~This has nothing to do with your former life, or has it? This is about you being a victim of the slave trade and helping to bring justice to the other victims—and for the safety of the city.~
@104  = ~All right, I get it! I get it!~
@105  = ~Well now, Shuck. I see you had your wish granted. It's time to go to work, then.~
@106  = ~I... I have nothing of importance to say! I swear!~
@107  = ~How about you let us decide that, hm?~
@108  = ~You are the tower's interrogator?~
@109  = ~I'll leave you to it, then.~
@110  = ~Indeed, Halfslinger, at your service.~
@111  = ~Please, don't be too cruel to him.~
@112  = ~Don't spare that criminal, Halfslinger.~
@113  = ~Now, how this goes is all up to you, Shuck, hm? Let's begin...~
@114  = ~Please, leave... so I can start working. I'll be available for buy and sell shortly in my house.~
@115  = ~Hello, there, traveller! There are many fine berries to find here, if you know where to look... Oh, it's you. Guys, we've got company!~
@116  = ~Hello, there, traveller! There are many fine berries to find here, if you know where to look. Of course, you have to know the right kind to pick the edible ones.~
@117  = ~All right, now that I actually have a suspicion about what you really are, this sounds awfully like a watchword. We aren't looking for berries, but for Shuck's brother. Does that mean anything to you?~
@118  = ~We are here in Shuck's name. Ring any bells?~
@119  = ~Well then, have fun looking for your berries.~
@120  = ~Is that so? Hm, then there is no need for the disguise, I guess. Guys, we've got company!~
@121  = ~I am sorry for the greeting, but you *accidentally* killed our business partner in the Seatower, so there is only one thing that will happen here now. I am sure you understand.~
@122  = ~Now there, why would you attack an innocent berry picker! You are a mean person, you know that?~
@123  = ~Who the hell are you?~
@124  = ~Bandits! I will kill you all!~
@125  = ~Berry picker, yeah right.~
@126  = ~So you seriously just attacked out of the blue?~
@127  = ~We are no bandits, we are *smugglers*. We do not steal, we deliver! On time and for a fair price! See that tent up there in the north? If we do not deliver black lotus there every day on time, a lot of people will become very irritated in a very short amount of time. So, please leave us alone. Who knows, maybe you'll have use for us somewhere in the future.~
@128  = ~Oh, now look at that! Shuck sent you, eh? Thinks he can throw a bunch of heavily armed mercenaries our way to get rid of the debts?~
@129  = ~Yes, we are the smugglers, in case you were wondering.~
@130  = ~I am here for Shuck's brother, yes. I have the gold you wanted.~
@131  = ~I am here for his brother, but you will not see any gold for it! Tell me where he is, now!~
@132  = ~I won't fight you. I am here because I want to work with you!~
@133  = ~I'm here because I want to fight. The more, the merrier!~
@134  = ~Oh, but don't you think you are rushing just a little bit? You don't even know how many of us there are yet!~
@135  = ~Fine, then I'll wait until I know how many of you there are.~
@136  = ~No, I will fight you now!~
@137  = ~Ho! I like you! You've got spine!~
@138  = ~A pity you are one of those do-gooders, or I'd have a use for you.~
@139  = ~A pity you travel with one of those do-gooders, I'd have use for you.~
@140  = ~*sigh* Here we go. Guys...~
@141  = ~Are you telling me you've got all the gold I requested right here?~
@142  = ~All right, all right, I get it. You are too strong for us. But killing us here won't help you find Shuck's brother now, would it? Please stop killing us, we are smugglers, not murderers or bandits. We do not steal, we deliver!~
@143  = ~I couldn't care less about that brother. I am here because I want to work with you!~
@144  = ~Stop talking. I'm not done with you!~
@145  = ~Oh, come *on*! Really?~
@146  = ~Let me introduce myself. I am Holger, and I am a smuggler. My mates and I smuggle illicit or very rare goods from Amn to the Sword Coast and Baldur's Gate. Let me play with open cards here. We know Shuck is in prison now, so it's only a matter of time until the tower interrogator learns this: Shuck was our Seatower contact. He told the tower cell when it would be safe to land and made sure the wares got into the city all right.~
@147  = ~Shuck did what?!~
@148  = ~Oh.~
@149  = ~He told me he needs to bail out his brother from you!~
@150  = ~A-haha, what a sly little soldier.~
@151  = ~And that is true!~
@152  = ~Ah, Shuck didn't tell you he worked for us, did he? Well, he did start working for us to bail out his brother, now that is true. Shuck's brother still owes us gold, so we requested that the debt be paid, at least. Well, Shuck paid, but too late.~
@153  = ~What do you mean "too late"?~
@154  = ~Fine. I have the 1,000 gold here to bail him out, so give me Shuck's brother.~
@155  = ~I don't care about your problems. Tell me where Shuck's brother is!~
@156  = ~Sounds like you could use some help, hm?~
@157  = ~Help? You mean from you. Yes, that is an intriguing thought.~
@158  = ~At least if I get myself to trust you. Hmm...~
@159  = ~Because we get a lot of pressure from all sides. It's not only us doing shady smuggler things, you know? Competition and all that, and other folks are much, much scarier than us. As for Shuck's brother...~
@160  = ~Well... See, we had to sell the brother's debts to someone else to get our gold in time. We told that fool he should hurry. What does he think we are? Ilmater monks? His brother got himself knee-deep into problems here... we are just businessmen who want to get our pay!~
@161  = ~Sold his debts? So where is he now?~
@162  = ~By the nine hells, spit it out already! Where is the brother now?~
@163  = ~Oh, now that hurt. I live and breathe by the smugglers' honor code! Don't you make fun of it. You'd wish all "criminals" would go by such a code, the world would be peaceful as a monastery—at least for those who can pay.~
@164  = ~Then tell me where I can find those slave traders!~
@165  = ~I am here to find Shuck's brother and I will not return without him!~
@166  = ~See, it's not *us* doing slavery, all right? We are just decent smugglers who provide paying customers with things they couldn't get otherwise... cheaply, that is. We just... need the traders for their ships as they carry some of our goods on their way from Amn to here, so we need to be good friends with them. And we needed the gold to pay off some very irritated customers. It was hard as it is with all the bandits around. These are difficult times for honest smugglers, you know?~
@167  = ~Honest smugglers? Don't make me laugh.~
@168  = ~We only sold that soldier's brother's *debts* to the slave traders because gold is scarce in these times of upcoming war and the iron crisis. A lot of our customers couldn't pay on time. We have obligations, too!~
@169  = ~You expect me to have sympathy with your problems?~
@170  = ~Very well, but I'm not done with you, believe me!~
@171  = ~Now that's settled, let's talk business, shall we?~
@172  = ~Then we are done here. Farewell.~
@173  = ~Well, taken by the slave traders. Probably being transported to a ship in the city's harbor as we speak. What can I say—I am sorry?~
@174  = ~That means the brother's debts are closed?~
@175  = ~Slave traders?!~
@176  = ~I respect your wish to keep your promise to Shuck. Fine.~
@177  = ~Take this, then. This is a contact note for one of their slave auctions. You could have gotten that from anywhere. I'm just sending a potential customer their way, right? No need for this *help* of mine to backfire to my disadvantage, no?~
@178  = ~I appreciate it.~
@179  = ~Your *help* wouldn't have been needed if you hadn't sold his brother in the first place!~
@180  = ~If not with us, then with our customers. What do you think the poor lot in that black lotus tent would do without us? Wait, you don't have to answer that. *You* probably think it's our fault they are in there in the first place!~
@181  = ~Now that's a reply I like to hear!~
@182  = ~Now, what would you mean by that, pray tell?~
@183  = ~Nothing. Just that I will have an eye on you and your kind, so better behave.~
@184  = ~(truth) Well, times are rough, as you already said. I wouldn't mind some extra business to earn some extra gold, if you know what I mean.~
@185  = ~(lie) You hinted at having a use for me. I want to work for you.~
@186  = ~I am saying now that I have the information I need, I will kill you! Draw your weapons!~
@187  = ~Indeed. Farewell to you, too.~
@188  = ~You really would like to work for me, hm?~
@189  = ~Look, I put a lot of trust into you already, all right? Even talking to a shiny metal suit like you alone is seen as treason by some. But I notice potential when I see it, so let's try this.~
@190  = ~I might have a job or two, but first you need to earn our trust. I need to see how you deal with the traders. Because in case you go there to stir trouble so that it will boomerang back on *us*, then we will not become business partners, that much is clear. Then I need to see how you fare at the tower.~
@191  = ~I might have a job or two, but first you need to earn our trust. I need to see how you fare at the tower.~
@192  = ~As I already said, the tower's interrogator will get to the information about us through Shuck eventually, anyway, so I don't mind you telling the Seatower commander all of *that*. But how you will go on from there is the real question. Because, if you want to work with me, you'd also want to work with the smuggler cell at the tower, or so I would assume. And there we have the first problem. Are you still in on this?~
@193  = ~Hmm. I know you helped Juan escape *and* you already solved the mystery of why the tower cell didn't communicate with us anymore, so we *could* say you already worked for me, and with a very important matter. Fine. I'd have one more task for you.~
@194  = ~Go on.~
@195  = ~Some weeks ago we lost contact with the tower cell—completely. Then the tower cell stopped delivering. We have complaints from all over Baldur's Gate about customers not receiving their wares! Shuck didn't contact us, either. We had to believe the cell was revealed, imprisoned, or killed, or all of the above. Actually, we suspected Shuck might have revealed us, but Shuck insists the tower cell stopped talking to *him*, and that he did nothing against us.~
@196  = ~So, let your contact to the Seatower authorities be to our advantage, then: Find out what happened to the smuggler cell that used to operate at Balduran's Tower. Were they taken out? Were they taken over by a rival group? Did the slave traders get too prickly and think they could do without us?~
@197  = ~I think I already know the answer to that, actually.~
@198  = ~I will do that.~
@199  = ~I guess I have no choice but to accept this job.~
@200  = ~I'm not sure I'll have time to deal with this soon.~
@201  = ~Oh? That's interesting. Nevertheless, let me finish what I wanted to say first.~
@202  = ~If you want to work for us, you'd better make time.~
@203  = ~Here's a very important delivery for the cook at the "Helm and Cloak" in northwest Baldur's Gate. Deliver this within the next three days, at the latest.~
@204  = ~The "Helm and Cloak" is getting smuggled wares? Aren't they scared of the Flaming Fist finding out about it?~
@205  = ~A sealed box? How can I trust this won't harm me?~
@206  = ~I want to know what is in this box if I am to deliver it for you.~
@207  = ~All right, I'm on my way.~
@208  = ~What if I don't want to do that?~
@209  = ~Oh, and we expect you not to open that box. The box is sealed, and we expect the seal to be unbroken upon delivery. It's a test, obviously.~
@210  = ~Do these two tasks—deliver the box and inquire what happened to the tower cell—and return to me with news. You will not regret it. Hurry with the delivery, though. You have three days.~
@211  = ~Do this task—deliver the box and return to me with news. You will not regret it. Hurry with the delivery, though—you have three days.~
@212  = ~Then I'll have to send someone else with the box and deeply regret having told you all the things I did. And if I regret something, I usually make the ones responsible regret it, too.~
@213  = ~Threatening me? You shouldn't have. I will kill you! Draw your weapons!~
@214  = ~You're not supposed to *tell* them, are you? And don't you judge our customers. What did I say: not all of our wares are illicit, some are just *rare*, all right?~
@215  = ~Ah, but no, you don't see the point here. It's not about *you* trusting *me*. The real question is: why would *I* trust *you*? Smuggling means you deliver wares you know as little about as possible. And you do that because you know I will give you wares that pay good gold and will not harm you, unless you get caught by the law, that is, but I don't need to explain why that's an exception now, do I?~
@216  = ~So, this is what this is about. You need to trust me if you want to work for me. But, more crucially, *I* must trust you with my wares. I guarantee that this box is not poisonous, nor will it explode, nor does it violate any general moral standards. As long as you keep it out of the city authorities' way, no harm will come to you from it.~
@217  = ~Work with us? Now, that's an interesting turn of events. How about we get to know each other first, then?~
@218  = ~Yes, for us, his debts are closed.~
@219  = ~(truth) Great, because I couldn't care less about the brother. I came here to get in contact with you. I could use some extra gold, as well.~
@220  = ~Oh, now that is a relief, considering we sold him already. Not him, mind you, but his *debts*, of course. But what can I do if the traders take him as a refund? So, we can put that whole affair to rest, then. At least I doubt we'll see many mercenaries coming our way from Shuck in the future...~
@221  = ~Now then, <CHARNAME>. You return—but what did you do? Attacked and killed the traders' contact after I gave you the information on where to find him!~
@222  = ~Well, you delivered the package to the customer, but—why did you tip off the Flaming Fist *and* show your face here again? Did you think we wouldn't know?~
@223  = ~You return from your first delivery!~
@224  = ~And a successful one it was. Our customer was pleased, and so am I. Well done!~
@225  = ~Someone couldn't hold their curiosity and had to look inside, hm?~
@226  = ~And *eating* parts of the delivery, <CHARNAME>?~
@227  = ~I have the feeling you do not understand the priority of these deliveries. We do not do them when we find the time, <CHARNAME>. They are *important* and *urgent*. You dawdled.~
@228  = ~Oh well, our customer was fine with it, and the gold he withheld was your share, so I will turn a blind eye to that—this once. This once, <CHARNAME>. Congratulations on your first job well done!~
@229  = ~*Figs*, Holger? Why?~
@230  = ~So, what's my next task?~
@231  = ~We are done here, I won't continue working for you.~
@232  = ~This is unacceptable behavior. I had high hopes for you, <CHARNAME>, such high hopes. But this will not do. Our business relationship is over. Farewell.~
@233  = ~And that is absolutely unacceptable behavior—which needs to be punished.~
@234  = ~Why yes, <CHARNAME>, figs are perfect for a recruit. They are quite sensitive, need to be delivered fast—and they are totally lawful. Take the mysterious, sealed package to the Flaming Fist, and the only person getting in trouble with them is you!~
@235  = ~Hm. Oh well, then it is time to say farewell, <CHARNAME>.~
@236  = ~Hm, let me see.~
@237  = ~There is still the question of what happened to the tower cell. Let's stick to that for now, as it is really important.~
@238  = ~I have to say, what did I hear—the tower cell taken over by doppelgangers! Juan was full of praise for your rescue mission.~
@239  = ~Sort of, since you tried to kill him nonetheless. Tsk, tsk, <CHARNAME>, you should know better than to threaten a fellow smuggler. Or you are smarter than you look and strive for a position at the top, hm?~
@240  = ~My contact from the slavers has stopped coming, <CHARNAME>. Did something happen?~
@241  = ~You can say that again, Holger. The slavers wanted to get back at you because they blame you for the fact that the smuggler's tunnel is no longer available. They didn't get very far after they told me that, though.~
@242  = ~Take a guess.~
@243  = ~You return. This is a good sign. You survived, at least. How did it go?~
@244  = ~You were right, they tried to enslave me the moment I walked in.~
@245  = ~They wanted to deliver a message. Unfortunately there is no one left to do so.~
@246  = ~As expected.~
@247  = ~Ah, I heard you also killed Leonhard. Well, I guess I can't blame you after the slavers broke our contract.~
@248  = ~Ah, they wanted to send a "greeting", didn't they? No one has arrived here, so you've done a great job. I suppose I owe you a debt of gratitude, <CHARNAME>.~
@249  = ~I thank you and must admit that I am grateful that you stood between me and the angry slavers, because I would not have survived their attack. Oh well.~
@250  = ~They... they attacked you? While you were working for them?—Please tell me this didn't have anything to do with me.~
@251  = ~Yeah, well. The slavers wanted to get back at you because they blame you for the fact that the smuggler's tunnel is no longer available. They didn't get very far after they told me that, though.~
@252  = ~"It didn't have anything to do with you." Satisfied?~
@253  = ~With the slavers' trade routes gone for us, everything here would have blown up in my face by now. I am glad that we are already dealing with repositioning ourselves. And I have to reposition myself, <CHARNAME>. I had really imagined our collaboration to be different.~
@254  = ~Does that mean you have nothing more for me to do?~
@255  = ~I fear you'll do that without me, Holger. I need to end our collaboration.~
@256  = ~Heh! I like your humor. So it *was* addressed to me?~
@257  = ~Not as much as I was hoping—but I'm actually very happy to have you battle-hardened recruits. We're not going to let something like that spoil our mood, are we?~
@258  = ~The slavers have requested a meeting, <CHARNAME>. I'll be honest. I'm not expecting anything good. It's unusual that they want to see me like this. I'd like to send you—because I'm not going there. It would be my death sentence, I already know that, and my rogues aren't strong enough to take them on.~
@259  = ~All right, I'll do it.~
@260  = ~Why would the slavers want to kill you?~
@261  = ~But you want me to let myself be slaughtered?~
@262  = ~You should face them like a man, Holger! Don't be such a coward.~
@263  = ~Hmm. I like you, but I'm not doing this for you, Holger.~
@264  = ~Nah, I wanted to end our collaboration, anyway.~
@265  = ~Oh, I can definitely read a room, <CHARNAME>. The smuggling tunnels to Baldur's Gate are lost, and that's hitting the slavers hard too. I wouldn't say I'm responsible, but I'm sure they'd have no problem blaming me, if only to save their own hides from their bosses.~
@266  = ~But quite the opposite. I want to send you there because I am convinced that *you* would come out of such a confrontation alive!~
@267  = ~I can't blame you. I'm going to disappear now. This means our collaboration is over, unfortunately. What a pity! I still have a lot to do, but this is getting too dicey for me. *sigh* So the smugglers' cell is now signing off completely. I never thought I'd let my customers down like this.~
@268  = ~All right, all right, enough of the theatrics. I'll confront your slavers for you.~
@269  = ~Farewell, Holger.~
@270  = ~If I faced them like a man, I'd be killed like a dog, <CHARNAME>.~
@271  = ~Luck really smiled upon me when she sent you my way. Meet them at the western shore of the Chionthar, south of Wyrm's Crossing, when night has fallen. Go there prepared, this *will* be a hostile confrontation. And—don't let yourself be killed, <CHARNAME>.~
@272  = ~You tipped off the Flaming Fist? *And* show your face again here. Did you think we wouldn't know?~
@273  = ~There you are. The second payment for the delivery already arrived. Well done, <CHARNAME>. This was a very lucrative job and largely filled the hole that the tower cell fiasco had torn in our finances.~
@274  = ~I could use your expertise with one more thing, if you are ready.~
@275  = ~There you are. The second payment for the delivery did not arrive. Instead, I was told you never showed with the promised delivery? You remember what I told you about trust—you trusting me, me trusting you, etcetera?~
@276  = ~Holger, I denied working with the slavers for a reason. You can't let me carry a person without a warning!~
@277  = ~Holger, you can't let me carry a person without a warning!~
@278  = ~I noticed a person inside a barrel who didn't respond to any attempts at communication—I was trying to secure the goods, Holger! Can't you see?~
@279  = ~Yes, <CHARNAME>, I can, because *I* am not a slaver!~
@280  = ~I don't care what your reasons for blowing this were. I lost trust in you, as sad as it is.~
@281  = ~<CHARNAME>, do you have any idea how much gold you've squandered? I had to spend the commission I got to compensate the contact, so I have no profit at all! That means I have just as much gold as before... (sighs) So, I guess it doesn't matter. Fine. I see your point. You were consistent in your actions until now, so I should probably take some of the blame.~
@282  = ~You are back—but I see no vial of Calishite fragrance. I did get reports about the Seatower Guard being more on alert with regard to smuggler activities as they already were, though.~
@283  = ~Yes, I'm sorry, Holger. I messed up.~
@284  = ~So you already know.~
@285  = ~Well, these things can happen, no?~
@286  = ~I sent you there because I thought it's a cakewalk for you, <CHARNAME>, and then you go and make the Seatower Guard *aware* of the vial?~
@287  = ~Well, retrieving it now would be a real challenge. One vial is not worth this amount of hassle. I'll just put it aside—as well as your failure. I guess it was wishful thinking on my part to leave this to you. I should have known better—and I mean that in all seriousness. I should have known better what kind of tasks to entrust you with. I apologize.~
@288  = ~You are back—and I see a vial of Calishite fragrance. You were successful!~
@289  = ~Let me take this off your hands and give you your pay. Ah—it's full and as good as new! My assumption regarding the actual *use* of this kind of present was correct, then. You might not know what this kind of fragrance is worth. This is a task well done, <CHARNAME>.~
@290  = ~I am not entirely *sure* what you did, but I was told the Seatower Guard seems to be on even higher alert with regard to smugglers than they already were. Well, I guess the tunnels are off limits even more in the near future.~
@291  = ~You are back, and from the noises I take it that the Night Masks are defeated. I thank you for this, I truly do and will give you your reward. But, <CHARNAME>—you attacked the soldiers? When I said you shouldn't let soldiers grab you with my goods, I meant run away or something like that! But not killing! Do you know what kind of repercussions this will have? Did you think dead soldiers so close to our base was a clever idea? I hate to break it to you that it wasn't—not at all.~
@292  = ~You are back—and from the noises I take it that the Night Masks are defeated. Well done, <CHARNAME>! Very well done. Here is your reward, and let me take back the crate.~
@293  = ~I'll give it to Edgar. *Now* we can put in the black lotus and deliver it for real.~
@294  = ~Oh, I see you opened it.~
@295  = ~I bribed soldiers, Holger. Do I get compensated?~
@296  = ~Yes, it was, <CHARNAME>.~
@297  = ~Why?~
@298  = ~Are you kidding me, Holger? First you make me deliver highly secret figs, then I risk my life for empty boxes?~
@299  = ~Lure the Night Masks out without actually threatening any wares. Smart!~
@300  = ~It doesn't work this way, Holger. You'll have to tell me what's inside the boxes you have me deliver!~
@301  = ~Take a step back and look at the full picture.~
@302  = ~I told you that the Night Masks *would* attack. It was a trap, <CHARNAME>, a trap for our enemies. And because of you, it not only snapped shut, but also crushed them really well. Don't say I lied to you—I said the Nightshades *wanted* to raid one of our shipments with black lotus. I never said I'd let them do that!~
@303  = ~You probably feel betrayed. I respect that assessment. But I did not betray your trust that I would assign you to important tasks, <CHARNAME>. It was clearly a combat mission when you accepted it. The empty crates were worth their volume in gold. Because through them, we taught the Night Masks a lesson. The threat from these vultures should be significantly weakened for some time. You've done a good job, <CHARNAME>, and well deserved your reward!~
@304  = ~I understand why—lure the Night Masks out without actually threatening any wares. Smart!~
@305  = ~No, I don't like being played like this. I fear our collaboration is over.~
@306  = ~I hope this cleared it up a little.~
@307  = ~It cleared it up plenty. I understand now.~
@308  = ~It did, but Holger, if I'll have to fight for empty boxes again I'll kill you.~
@309  = ~Whoa! I surely hope you didn't mean that literally. But rest assured I will not let you protect empty boxes again. You have my word.~
@310  = ~That was exactly what this was about.~
@311  = ~I'm glad we're on the same page. I wouldn't have liked a misunderstanding between us.~
@312  = ~I'm glad I could be of service. What now?~
@313  = ~Go celebrate this victory. See you soon, <CHARNAME>—whenever you are ready!~
@314  = ~You managed to do that? This is brilliant! But it is, I'm afraid, occupational hazard—you need to *avoid* them next time. I do believe my pay is high enough to compensate your expenses.~
@315  = ~The crate was empty?~
@316  = ~Just give me my pay.~
@317  = ~I'm sorry, Holger, but I need to end our business relations.~
@318  = ~<CHARNAME>, in case it was not clear—you can rest assured that you won't transport anything that would really get you into trouble with the Flaming Fist. Do you seriously think I'm going to give you, a newbie, the really hot stuff? Well, another organization might do that to spare their own people. But I'm a smuggler, <CHARNAME>! If I don't supply my customers, my reputation suffers. Of course I give the dicey stuff to my experienced rogues. I'm not a light-headed man who wants to make quick gold and sell his people for it!~
@319  = ~You have to bear in mind that every failed delivery means a customer who doesn't receive their goods. Well, except for the empty box, of course. But that had a different purpose.~
@320  = ~That's *bait*, <CHARNAME>! Do you seriously think I'd use the real thing when I can get to it without risk? Go see if the Night Masks will bite. We'll discuss this later.~
@321  = ~Now we can—wait, what is going on?~
@322  = ~Oh, bugger. You called this in, <CHARNAME>, didn't you?~
@323  = ~We busted you, you scoundrel! We caught you red-handed with this flask of Calishite fragrance, illegally imported and sold without paying duties! You'll come with us now—and you better behave!~
@324  = ~<CHARNAME>, <CHARNAME>... You played me skillfully. What a pity you were on the other side all this time.~
@325  = ~<CHARNAME>—come to me later and collect your reward!~
@326  = ~*sigh* Hello, officers. How may I help you?~
@327  = ~We busted you, you scoundrel! We caught you red-handed with this box filled with—uh... nothing? The box is empty!~
@328  = ~It is, indeed! This wonderful <PRO_MANWOMAN> has volunteered, so I thought it would be a good thing to help *the honorable Nashkel guard* rid the area of some criminals! This is all that happened here, dear officer. Is there a reward for actions like these, if I may ask?~
@329  = ~This... this—this will not do! There is nothing here—*nothing*! <CHARNAME>, this was a failure. Your information was worth nothing! We will be leaving now. I'll lead the whole investigation myself from now on!~
@330  = ~Well, <CHARNAME>, I guess it'll be no surprise that I'll be leaving now, too.~
@331  = ~<CHARNAME>, I am not sure what happened, but as long as your likeness hangs at every second corner in Baldur's Gate, I guess it is best to pause our cooperation until things quiet down. Not because I would distance myself from you, but because being chased by the Flaming Fist and smuggling do not go well together.~
@332  = ~Well then, ready for some more action?~
@333  = ~Thanks for the smuggler's greeting, Holger. It was nice to see a friendly face in those turbulent times.~
@334  = ~I didn't think you had it in you to be involved in actual human trafficking, Holger! I see why it comes easy to you to make dealings with the slave traders.~
@335  = ~You know I am not averse to the thought of slave trading, but I do admit I was surprised when I realized that there was a person inside the barrel.~
@336  = ~Holger, I refused to cooperate with the slavers for a reason. Do you have any idea how much you have tested my loyalty by letting me smuggle a person unprepared?~
@337  = ~I was very surprised when I noticed a person inside the barrel, Holger. Are you expanding your trade?~
@338  = ~That transport contact for the heavy barrel—it seems you did not lose all your routes via ship together with the slavers?~
@339  = ~Yes. What do you need me to do?~
@340  = ~Yes. I'm ready to guard your meeting.~
@341  = ~Maybe later.~
@342  = ~So, do you have any news for me?~
@343  = ~The tower cell was taken over by doppelgangers, apparently.~
@344  = ~The "black lotus" box is empty, Holger!~
@345  = ~Are you kidding me? First I deliver highly secret figs, and now you let me guard empty boxes with my life?~
@346  = ~I'm still on it.~
@347  = ~I'm sorry, Holger, but I won't face the Night Masks, after all. Please take back the crate.~
@348  = ~I am still a smuggler, nothing more. The customer paid to have his wares delivered in secret—the "wares" being himself. It was all voluntary and of their own free will. And, I might add, also not for covering up a crime. At least if you don't consider diplomatic tensions to be illegal.~
@349  = ~I see your point. You were consistent in your actions until now, so I should probably have given you a hint.~
@350  = ~Appreciated. Yes, I was worried about seeing your likeness for a bounty all over the place. But you found your way back into the city—and quite impressively, if I may say so!~
@351  = ~Wh—<CHARNAME>, I kept my distance because I am no match for the Night Masks. You handing me this crate now means I'll disappear from here as fast as possible.~
@352  = ~Yes, sorry.~
@353  = ~Fine, I'll face them.~
@354  = ~Oh, no, not at all. The slavers were one of our contacts. I only cooperated because their offers were always so incredibly cost-efficient. And because they ran on some routes for which I had no other means, or at least not without taking considerable detours.~
@355  = ~Doppelgangers!~
@356  = ~Well, no wonder they no longer communicated with us.~
@357  = ~Hm, seems I did Shuck wrong, the poor man! I am grief-stricken—or maybe not.~
@358  = ~Well, you have proven useful, <CHARNAME>. Very much so.~
@359  = ~I like you and see that you know how to handle your weapons. Our... partners are always in need of mercenaries to protect their shipments.~
@360  = ~I am talking about the *helpful* folks with their base in Amn who have enough gold to buy off the debts of our customers and enough ships to transport some of our goods.~
@361  = ~In other words: the slavers. You already met their contact for that friend's brother of yours.~
@362  = ~In other words: they are slavers. There is no need to talk around the truth if I want you to work for them.~
@363  = ~We need to be on good terms with them, and they are often interested in backup against the Flaming Fist for their deliveries near Baldur's Gate. I will recommend you to them. It's easy earned gold. It's seldom that the Flaming Fist turns up at all, and the pay is usually quite good. I'd do it myself, but we will be busy with own stuff for a while.~
@364  = ~Finally! I was waiting for your recommendation towards them.~
@365  = ~Fine, I will listen to their offer.~
@366  = ~Absolutely not. Working for you is all right, but I will not be getting involved with any shady organization.~
@367  = ~Don't bother. I'm done working for you, as well.~
@368  = ~Don't let me wait too long for more news, <CHARNAME>.~
@369  = ~I have a serious problem. As if losing men in Baldur's Gate wasn't enough, I also lost rogues between Baldur's Gate and Nashkel. Someone is killing them, to an extent that I put certain deliveries on hold. This is one of the reasons why the loss of the tower cell took so long to be noticed.~
@370  = ~Don't tell me they're replaced by doppelgangers, too.~
@371  = ~Someone? Do you know who it is?~
@372  = ~I'd assume I wouldn't get to find their corpses then.~
@373  = ~I know it is the Night Masks and they are not afraid to make this clear. They seem to want to destroy my black lotus route. I need you to be my next smugglers on that route. I'll give you big, big boxes the Night Masks can't miss. This would be a fighting assignment, <CHARNAME>, because they will attack you.~
@374  = ~So I'd be bait?~
@375  = ~Hmm, Night Masks. Fine, I'll do it.~
@376  = ~Night Masks—what are they?~
@377  = ~What if I say no?~
@378  = ~The boxes will be bait. You will be the defense, and a capable one, I'd say.~
@379  = ~Then I will bury my black lotus smuggle to Baldur's Gate. I lost too many men already.~
@380  = ~I'm sorry, but I'll have to pass on that one.~
@381  = ~Great. Meet up with Edgar behind the black lotus tent at nightfall. He'll give you the precious loot and tell you where to go with it to attract the plunderers. You will return to me, <CHARNAME>—as soon as you've defeated whoever will be attacking the delivery. Make them remember we are not defenseless.~
@382  = ~It's basically an organization of elite assassins. They carry out contract killings and have started to get a little too involved in the area.~
@383  = ~I see. Well, then this has to wait, because I do not have anyone else as capable as you to send there. But fine, I have enough other things to keep me busy.~
@384  = ~I did not get payment for several of my deliveries. Considering your connections, I'd like to send you after one of them.~
@385  = ~Do you want me to collect your gold now? I wanted to work as a smuggler, not a bounty hunter.~
@386  = ~Sure, who am I supposed to beat up?~
@387  = ~What do you want me to do?~
@388  = ~No, no, don't worry, <CHARNAME>. You won't have anything to do with the client. Not if you can help it, at least.~
@389 = ~I want you to get a special item back for me: a Calimshan fragrance. It should be with the captain of the guard in the Seatower.~
@390  = ~Holger, are you trying to make me believe that the captain bought smuggled goods?!~
@391  = ~The Seatower guard? Are you sure?~
@392  = ~Oof. I think that's too delicate for me. I'm sorry, Holger.~
@393  = ~No, certainly not the captain.~
@394  = ~His people got the item through Shuck, a gift for their boss—you see? I think there are a lot of innocent people here, and Shuck really has enough problems already. Especially because it might not be Shuck's fault that I haven't received any gold.~
@395  = ~That would be unfortunate.~
@396  = ~I still have enough rogues to get most of my unpaid wares back. But from the Seatower barracks of all places... Well, let's put it this way, I'd rather send you there. You seem to visit the place quite unhindered.~
@397  = ~I'm sorry, Holger, but I won't do this.~
@398  = ~What a real pity. But well, I can't force you. Let me know if you're ready for another task.~
@399  = ~Great! I'm sure he kept it in the barracks out of respect for his men who gave it to him. I'm sure you'll find an unobserved moment to retrieve it.~
@400  = ~I have one very delicate delivery through the former smuggler tunnels under the Seatower. Given your unimpeded access to this tunnel, you would be perfect for it.~
@401  = ~You *might* want to stock up on strength enhancements, though.~
@402  = ~The barrel is a bit heavy. This time, there is something precious inside, you have my word. Something that needs to be handled with great care so not to break it. You must deliver it fast and not reveal the contents under any circumstances—these are the client's explicit orders. Can I count on you?~
@403  = ~Yes, I can do that.~
@404  = ~I'm sorry, Holger, but I don't think I can do this.~
@405  = ~Wonderful. The barrel will be in front of the tailor shop—you remember, where you met the sad remains of the former tower cell in the cellar. Carry it through the tunnels outside to the coast under the Seatower. My contacts will take it from you there. And, <CHARNAME>—this is a time-sensitive assignment. Lives are at stake—literally. Not to mention that my transport contact won't wait forever.~
@406  = ~I'd need your fighting prowess again, <CHARNAME>. I need you to guard a meeting I'll have. It's a very important one. I told you we need to reorganize ourselves. I'm not sure what or who will try to disturb us—Nashkel Guard, Night Masks, bears—I need you to bribe the former and kill the latter two. It might even be that I'll need your help against my visitors! Let me know when you are ready.~
@407  = ~Brilliant. Just stand here and be ready—for anything. Do not disturb us, but I assume that goes without saying.~
@408  = ~We are taking a short break. Everything still all right here?~
@409  = ~Yes, nothing happened so far.~
@410  = ~How did negotiations go so far?~
@411  = ~Will it take long still?~
@412  = ~It takes as long as it takes, I'm afraid.~
@413  = ~Keep it up!~
@414  = ~Not bad, not bad at all.~
@415  = ~And—we are done. Everything stayed quiet here?~
@416  = ~What do you mean, 'done'? Nothing happened!~
@417  = ~Wait—that's it? No fight, no quarrel, no carnage, no suddenly appearing enemies?~
@418  = ~Yes, everything stayed quiet here.~
@419  = ~If I had known that this meeting would be so uneventful, I would have spent my time differently.~
@420  = ~No, nothing of the sort.~
@421  = ~Had I known this meeting would be so uneventful, I wouldn't have bothered you, <CHARNAME>. Hmm, but you never know, you know.~
@422  = ~I'm sure your mere presence has greatly reduced any attempts by our business partners to rip us off. And the fact that no Night Masks turned up—well, not everything always has to go wrong, does it? For once, a secret meeting actually remained a secret meeting. I think that's a good sign for this new collaboration I am forming. You've done a very good job, <CHARNAME>.~
@423  = ~It is downright regrettable that I have to pause our smuggling cooperation at this point, as my duties now call me back to the Kings' Tears mother guild. The changing contingencies and new possibilities must be weighed up wisely, this discussion does not work well via carrier pigeons.~
@424  = ~I would love to hire you as my bodyguard and take you with me, but I know you have obligations elsewhere. If there is any chance, then we will meet again.~
@425  = ~I'd not waste such an asset as you, you can believe me that. Be assured that you made a very profitable business contact here—I'd say "friend", but we both know that wouldn't be the truth.~
@426  = ~Farewell, <CHARNAME>.~
@427  = ~You will hear from me when they tell us more details.~
@428  = ~Oh, what a pity. Guess I need to send Edgar then, again.~
@429  = ~The losses are a hard blow for us. I still have some orders I need to deliver, but I'll also start reorganizing my operations.~
@430  = ~If you are ready, I'd have more jobs for you to do. Just say the word.~
@431  = ~I'm just here for the muscle. Talk to Holger.~
@432  = ~Oh, I didn't notice the box you have there. That's for me, right? Now, let me see.~
@433  = ~You are late, you know that, yes?~
@434  = ~What's this—it's already opened?! Are you trying to trick me? I paid for Calishite figs, now I can't be sure you didn't switch them with cheap Tethyr ones!~
@435  = ~And it's no longer six! Some are missing!~
@436  = ~And—ugh, they are rotten. So you bring me some rotten figs instead of the promised fresh Calishite ones.~
@437  = ~I am very disappointed. Very. I will not pay you, as you haven't delivered what I ordered. Get out of my kitchen. Fresh delivery, pfeh.~
@438  = ~Hm, well, I guess I will accept the broken seal, they do look right. I *will* report this with my next order, however.~
@439  = ~Ah, yes, here they are: six marvellous Calishite figs.~
@440  = ~So here's something extra for you, and now get out of my kitchen, I have work to do.~
@441  = ~Hold! You are caught in the act of buying smuggled wares! It might just be figs... but they were smuggled! Pay a penalty fee of 1,000 gold coins. If we find you engaging in illegal activities again, we will not be so lenient!~
@442  = ~What—ugh, there it goes, the "saved" gold because it was so much cheaper, and then some! I shouldn't have let myself be talked into this. And *you*—don't think I'm so daft not to know it was you who tipped off the Flaming Fist!~
@443  = ~Now everyone out of my kitchen! Out, out, OUT!~
@444  = ~There you are, our new muscle. Ready?~
@445  = ~Yes. What am I to do?~
@446  = ~Not quite. I'll be back in a minute.~
@447  = ~Sure, I'll be here.~
@448  = ~First, here's the box. Just take this and walk north from here, in the direction of Baldur's Gate. But do it without haste, and especially, not in the shadows or even invisible! This is not about arriving, this is about luring them devils out. Once you're done with the Night Masks, just return to Holger.~
@449  = ~What can I say—do you think we are dumb and blind? So that box is empty. Well played! Well, except for the part where you stumbled all over the place with an empty box. We decided to kill you anyway—and I brought a few more friends with me, because you wanted to play dirty too.~
@450  = ~Thank you for the black lotus delivery. We'll take it from here.~
@451  = ~Who are you? Are you killing the smugglers?~
@452  = ~I don't think so.~
@453  = ~Please, just take the crate and let me go. This is not my fight!~
@454  = ~That's exactly what we are doing. How about we show you?~
@455  = ~Oh, but it is now!~
@456  = ~Ugh, a "hero".~
@457  = ~Well then, let's dance!~
@458  = ~There you are!~
@459  = ~[BG:EE #8907]I'll box your ears if you step an inch closer!~
@460  = ~Wait—wait, wait. I recognize <PRO_HIMHER> now. It's Telmov's smuggler spy! Why do you escalate the situation like this? Just tell us that you are on one of Telmov's missions!~
@461  = ~Hold! What happened here? Explain yourself!~
@462  = ~Well, what can I say—these here were Night Masks, and we were attacked by them!~
@463  = ~I am <CHARNAME>, I am working with Sergeant Telmov to reveal the smugglers. This is one of the missions.~
@464  = ~I'm an adventurer on my way to Baldur's Gate.~
@465  = ~I couldn't care less what you soldier pansies think of me!~
@466  = ~Oh, but you should, citizen!~
@467  = ~We were in pursuit of Night Masks who are said to have been roaming the area. It is late, and your presence here is dubious at best. What is this crate?~
@468  = ~Open it at once!~
@469  = ~No, please... ugh, I guess I'm caught. I really don't want to fight soldiers.~
@470  = ~Look, this is just my travel luggage. You don't want to go through my dirty laundry, do you?~
@471  = ~Of course, please do so. I am sure there is nothing suspicious to see. (At least I'd assume Holger would make sure of that...)~
@472  = ~Well, how about I'll give you, say, 500 gp and we'll just pretend this is not a suspicious crate I'm carrying around at night?~
@473  = ~Well, how about I'll give you, say, 300 gp and we'll just pretend this is not a suspicious crate I'm carrying around at night?~
@474  = ~Well, how about I'll give you, say, 100 gp and we'll just pretend this is not a suspicious crate I'm carrying around at night?~
@475  = ~If it comes to this, then draw your weapons! I won't surrender this crate to you!~
@476  = ~Open it, or we'll do it by force!~
@477  = ~A mission? He didn't tell us about it. Is he even aware?~
@478  = ~He did mention his smuggler spy, though.~
@479  = ~Right, he mentioned something. Fine, we'll do nothing so we don't risk busting the operation, then!~
@480  = ~Yes, best we leave the box untouched. I wouldn't be able to explain why you wouldn't confiscate a box of black lotus, I guess.~
@481  = ~On the contrary, I'd like to use the situation and open the box. We need to know what is in there if we want to use it to bust the smugglers.~
@482  = ~True words. Well, let's see...~
@483  = ~The crate is empty! Well, seems like Sergeant Telmov doesn't have much luck with busting those smugglers, eh.~
@484  = ~Are you trying to bribe us?!~
@485  = ~Well, they were headed north... As soon as they're out of Amn, does it really matter what is inside the crate?~
@486  = ~You mean we should be *thankful* someone brings what we think is black lotus *away* from here—and to the morons in Baldur's Gate so they'll be able to smoke themselves into brain loss?~
@487  = ~Duh?~
@488  = ~Hmph, fine.~
@489  = ~But make it 300 gold coins, at least!~
@490  = ~300 gold coins, here you are.~
@491  = ~500 gold coins, here you are.~
@492  = ~I don't have that much gold with me, as it seems.~
@493  = ~Then open the crate, now!~
@494  = ~I was afraid you'd say that.~
@495  = ~The crate is empty! Is this a joke? Why are you carrying an empty crate late at night?!~
@496  = ~That... is a very good question.~
@497  = ~It is *empty*? Holger, you damned...~
@498  = ~Well, someone lied to us too. I would have expected it to have been at least a big load of delicious figs. Or stones! Stones are also very popular.~
@499  = ~See? No contraband in there. Will you leave us alone now?~
@500  = ~Well, you see—the crate *is* the treasure. Have you never heard of the, er, very sturdy crates from Chult? They're all the rage in Baldur's Gate!~
@501  = ~Disappointed you didn't find a reason to attack us, eh?~
@502  = ~You think this is a joke?~
@503  = ~Watch your mouth!~
@504  = ~Hmph.~
@505  = ~I guess you can go, then. No trouble out of you!~
@506  = ~You made a mistake!~
@507  = ~Greetings. The bandit activities have been reduced significantly, but we still have problems with scoundrels of all sorts taking advantage of the situation. Let me know if you notice anything suspicious or hear of any bandit or smuggling activity. There are always rogues on the loose!~
@508  = ~You! I can't prove it, but I *know* it was you who killed my soldiers in pursuit of the Night Masks. Get out of my sight—be glad I'm too busy to haul you to prison until you confess on your own!~
@509  = ~<CHARNAME>! Here is your well-earned reward. This town is a little safer now, thanks to you. I will go on patrol again. Have a nice day!~
@510  = ~My comrades told me they met you on a mission! Why didn't I know about this?~
@511  = ~I am telling you now. I'll bring the smugglers' box to the smugglers' leader now. This will be a good opportunity to bust the smugglers.~
@512  = ~Don't bother, sergeant. The box is empty, it was really only bait for the Night Masks.~
@513  = ~It was a fake operation to draw the Night Masks out. The area is much safer again, but for busting the smugglers it wouldn't have worked.~
@514  = ~You have to trust me in this, or collaboration won't work.~
@515  = ~I see. Keep me updated.~
@516  = ~You make it hard to trust you, <CHARNAME>.~
@517  = ~Don't walk around with contraband again, or I will seriously reconsider our agreement.~
@518  = ~You make it hard to trust you, <CHARNAME>. Too hard, in fact.~
@519  = ~You keep on saying that, but I reached the end of my patience, <CHARNAME>.~
@520  = ~<CHARNAME>! What happened to our planned smuggler bust with regard to the smuggled goods you were retrieving from Baldur's Gate?~
@521  = ~I even gave you an official document for it!~
@522  = ~Seems I forgot to tell you how it went—now that smuggling heist is finished.~
@523  = ~Erm, oops. Sorry...~
@524  = ~<CHARNAME>! So much time has passed, but you have not provided any helpful leads.~
@525  = ~I can't wait for you any longer. Our collaboration with regard to the smugglers now comes to an end.~
@526  = ~Have you witnessed any bandit or smuggler activity you'd like to report?~
@527  = ~I would like to report a smuggler organization having their meeting point near here. Lieutenant Bardolan said I should talk to you about it.~
@528  = ~I would like to report a smuggler organization having their meeting point near here.~
@529  = ~(lie) You know, you won't find much when confronting smugglers unprepared, and observation takes time and manpower. I could be your spy with them—I pretended I wanted to work with them. This way I could inform you when they are planning real heists, so you could catch them red-handed.~
@530  = ~I'll end my spying with the smugglers. If you want to take them out, you need to go to their meeting place, southwest of the black lotus tent at the carnival.~
@531  = ~There was a smuggler organization working from Amn, but they are gone for now.~
@532  = ~The smuggler organization working from Amn is gone now.~
@533  = ~There was a smuggler meeting point near here, southwest of the black lotus tent at the carnival. They are history now, though. I found this box with them. I think it is a delivery for an inn in Baldur's Gate.~
@534  = ~There was a smuggler meeting point near here, southwest of the black lotus tent at the carnival. They are history now, though.~
@535  = ~The smugglers gave me this suspicious box I'm supposed to bring to Baldur's Gate. It's a first test job, so I guess it would be best if I did this to get on their good side. I just wanted to let you know.~
@536  = ~I'm supposed to carry a delivery to lure out Night Masks who are targeting black lotus smuggling activities, but I will bring it back to the smugglers' leader afterward. This might be a good opportunity to confront them in the act.~
@537  = ~Good that you're still here. Do not come to the smugglers' place! The box is empty—there is nothing inside. It really was just bait for the Night Masks.~
@538  = ~Would it be possible to get something official stating that I am actually working for you? It would definitely make the retrieval of the smuggler item much easier.~
@539  = ~(truth) The next smuggler heist is up. I am supposed to get wares back from Baldur's Gate. I just wanted to let you know so you can get ready to bust the smugglers once I bring it back.~
@540  = ~(lie) The next smuggler heist is up. I am supposed to get wares back from Baldur's Gate and return them to the smugglers. I just wanted to let you know and ask whether I can get your support in any way.~
@541  = ~I am back from Baldur's Gate with the smuggled goods. I'll go and confront the smugglers now. Will you be there?~
@542  = ~I am back from Baldur's Gate with smuggled goods. I'll go and confront the smugglers now. This is your chance to get them red-handed!~
@543  = ~There won't be any smuggler bust. I'm sorry, it didn't go as planned in Baldur's Gate, so I have nothing to hand over to the smugglers.~
@544  = ~What is your status here?~
@545  = ~I didn't see you here before. Are you newly stationed to Nashkel?~
@546  = ~Good day to you.~
@547  = ~All right, that's a pity.~
@548  = ~The local smuggler threat is dealt with then, I take it. Thank you for the report, citizen.~
@549  = ~Very good. Make sure you let us know once you return, before you give anything compromising back to the smugglers, so that we can confront them.~
@550  = ~Would it be possible to get something official stating that I am actually working for you? It would definitely make the retrieval of the smuggler item much easier. It is inside the Seatower barracks, of all places.~
@551  = ~I'll be on my way.~
@552  = ~Night Masks! So the scoundrels make life difficult for each other. What will the delivery be?~
@553  = ~I don't know.~
@554  = ~Black lotus—duh?~
@555  = ~Do you want to take a look?~
@556  = ~I would, but I'm afraid it's best if we don't tamper with the box so as not to arouse suspicion.~
@557  = ~Hmm, it could be black lotus, indeed, if it is supposed to be bait.~
@558  = ~We can't help you against the Night Masks since this would look suspicious.~
@559  = ~We will be there when you return to the smugglers with the wares. Let's finish this.~
@560  = ~Good that you gave me a warning! I'll wait to confront the smugglers, then.~
@561  = ~Do you know what's inside?~
@562  = ~No, it's a test. He didn't tell me on purpose.~
@563  = ~Not the slightest idea.~
@564  = ~It's figs.~
@565  = ~If I knew, I wouldn't have said "I have a box here", would I now?~
@566  = ~Does it matter?~
@567  = ~In this case you'd best inform our counterpart in Baldur's Gate so they can catch the people involved when you hand it over!~
@568  = ~Would it be possible to get something official stating that I am actually working for you? I feel reluctant to walk around with smuggler wares in my backpack. Approaching the Flaming Fist would also be a bit easier if they could see this is part of an official investigation.~
@569  = ~I think I can handle that.~
@570  = ~Figs?~
@571  = ~Opening this box would indeed lead to nothing, since we can't prove it was handed to you by the smugglers.~
@572  = ~I am Sergeant Telmov. I make sure that our lieutenant's orders are carried out and I ensure the training and discipline of the men under our command.~
@573  = ~No, on the contrary. The calming of the bandit situation has made it possible for me to return here, I was previously on assignment. Now I'll have more time to address common criminals besides bandits.~
@574  = ~Smugglers, you say? We did notice some activity, but we weren't sure exactly where they were operating from. Despite the number of soldiers, we don't have the needed manpower to investigate every suspicious incident.~
@575  = ~Their meeting point is southwest of the black lotus tent at the carnival. There is usually someone there, disguised as a berry picker. He's the smuggler's contact.~
@576  = ~(truth) I let myself be hired by them, but I only did so to be able to get more insight into their operations. I will keep you informed.~
@577  = ~On second thought, I am not sure. I'll go now.~
@578  = ~Yes, it fits with other reports about suspicious activity we've received.~
@579  = ~If we can't catch them, we can at least make their operation much harder. Thank you for the report, citizen.~
@580  = ~Oh? News like these are music to my ears.~
@581  = ~Usually, news like these are music to my ears, but in this case I was hoping we'd be able to see more activity to catch them red-handed.~
@582  = ~Very well, thank you for letting us know.~
@583  = ~You have proven yourself when freeing the mines. I will trust you on this one. For the time being, we will not take action on our own against the smugglers but wait for your reports on when to hit them hard. You'll let me know if there will be any smuggling activity that we could use to catch them in the act.~
@584  = ~Hmm, some official document, you say? Look, I get where you're coming from, but you basically asked me to certify a smuggling operation.~
@585  = ~With due respect, but this won't happen. Be glad I let you walk with that box at all!~
@586  = ~Hmm, but you do have a point there. If we can take out their middlemen this will help us in the end, too.~
@587  = ~Fine. Here, take this—but if I hear you misused this in any way, I'll hunt you down just like I will the smugglers!~
@588  = ~Hmm, I do admit that you are, in a way, an agent on a mission to our favor.~
@589  = ~It's still a bit much to ask. I still do not really know you, all I have is your word. I hope you understand that I don't see myself handing out such a document to you yet.~
@590  = ~Good day, citizen.~
@591  = ~I'll also take back the collaboration document.~
@592  = ~I'm not sure why I gave it to you in the first place!~
@593  = ~That is good information. Go to the barracks and let Sergeant Telmov know, he'll discuss the details.~
@594  = ~Greetings. With the bridge down, we have more riffraff inside the city again. Good thing the reduced bandit activities made it possible that more mercenaries could return to headquarters. We will need them to handle the criminals trying their luck inside the Gates again. Do not hesitate to report any suspicious or illegal activities you witness. We will have our hands full, but the safety of the city comes first.~
@595  = ~Ah, you were the ones revealing the smuggler tunnels beneath the Seatower, were you not? In this case, if you know of any more smuggler activities, come to me at once!~
@596  = ~Ah, <CHARNAME>. Here is your reward for reporting the cook's smuggler activities. This went rather well. Keep us informed!~
@597  = ~I'll also add a confirmation about your help with this bust. I figure it could help secure the Nashkel guard's support—and if the organizing smugglers are gone there, activities here will cease, too.~
@598  = ~Do you have anything to report?~
@599  = ~(truth) I let myself be hired by smugglers operating in Baldur's Gate, but I only did so to be able to get more insight into their operations. I will keep you informed.~
@600  = ~I have this sealed box with smuggled wares I am supposed to bring to a cook in the Helm and Cloak inn.~
@601  = ~I have this sealed box with smuggled wares from smugglers I busted. I think it was supposed to go to the cook in the Helm and Cloak inn.~
@602  = ~I have knowledge about a smuggler heist in the Seatower.~
@603  = ~I have knowledge of slave traders doing their business right from *inside* the Seatower!~
@604  = ~Nothing at the moment.~
@605  = ~Hmm, this is Seatower territory. In this case, please report to the Captain of the Seatower Guard directly, so he can discuss the details with you.~
@606  = ~I am Manip Limmon. In case you're not familiar with the terminology, a manip is what you would call a sergeant in other military organizations. I am under direct command of our flame—our lieutenant—and help them command the fists—our soldiers. I was ordered to deal with gang delinquencies and organized illegal activities inside the Gates.~
@607  = ~A smuggler delivery! What is inside?~
@608  = ~It's just figs... but they were smuggled, nonetheless.~
@609  = ~Actually, this is a test on which the smugglers will decide whether they'll trust me or not. I know this is hard to ask, but would you turn a blind eye to this one and let me become a full member first?~
@610  = ~Is there a way for you not to interfere so that my "mission" doesn't get compromised? Like, let it seem like you got a tip from somewhere else and raid the Helm and Cloak long after I'm gone? This way, I'll be able to deliver better intel in the long run.~
@611  = ~Knowing the contents would help in evaluating the severity of this legal offense.~
@612  = ~What are you implying?~
@613  = ~Smuggled goods are smuggled goods, no?~
@614  = ~There is no way to look now without breaking the seal of this box. It could jeopardize the operation to catch the cook in the Helm and Cloak in the act.~
@615  = ~Well, then let's have a look, shall we?~
@616  = ~I do have an official cooperation pass from the Nashkel guard, if that means anything.~
@617  = ~Of course!~
@618  = ~Opening it would risk raising suspicion before we go in, that's for sure. Hmm.~
@619  = ~There's a difference between a rare and protected magical ingredient and something someone smuggled just to save a few gold coins.~
@620  = ~But I am *not* implying that there are instances where pursuing illegal activities would not be worth it! I was trying to estimate the scale of this transaction, which would determine the manpower required to disrupt it.~
@621  = ~So, will you be there when I hand over the box?~
@622  = ~Seeing as I probably already compromised this test mission, the best thing we can do is to catch the cook in the Helm and Cloak, at least.~
@623  = ~Is there a chance to leave me out of this? Like, raid the Helm and Cloak long after I am gone?~
@624  = ~We will be there when you hand over the box.~
@625  = ~Hmm. Letting a report of an illegal action slide for the "greater good"?~
@626  = ~And why would I do that?~
@627  = ~Like you just said—because you'll gain more in the long run by not "compromising" me now.~
@628  = ~Just make sure the smugglers do not know it was me who reported them so they do not lose faith in me. I am all for the people at the Helm and Cloak who are responsible getting their proper punishment.~
@629  = ~Just make sure the smugglers do not know it was me who reported them. I am all for the people at the Helm and Cloak who are responsible getting their proper punishment.~
@630  = ~I already gave you the hint. I have no interest in being involved in the punishment, you know.~
@631  = ~You are right, it's a bit much to ask and also rather suspicious, I guess.~
@632  = ~Let me see. Hmm. Yes, this means a lot.~
@633  = ~You won't be.~
@634  = ~Fine, we will hold our horses until you are long out of sight. The cook at the Helm and Cloak will not know it was you who gave us the information. Come after a week or so to collect your reward!~
@635  = ~You've already opened the box. Do you really think they'd still trust you?~
@636  = ~All right, that's something I like to hear.~
@637  = ~So now they're gone? It's a shame we couldn't find a way to catch them when they were still active, but it's still good to hear.~
@638  = ~So now they're gone? All right, that's something I like to hear. Thank you for your cooperation regarding this. We might have not been able to crush the full organization here inside the city, but you helped us identifying some of the structures. Farewell.~
@639  = ~Hmm, I think I know who you are referring to. That would be Seatower Commander Airard's area of control. Let him know if you have any more details regarding this. I know he wants to convict the bastards.~
@640  = ~Move on, then.~
@641  = ~Hmm, fine. You have made a name for yourself already, and I know Scar was looking for you. Keep me informed about any smuggler activities you know of. Busting them would be ideal, but I also take hints regarding their customers.~
@642  = ~I would like to report a smuggler organization from Amn operating in Baldur's Gate.~
@643  = ~Ah. Our manip would be the right address for these sorts of reports—Manip Limmon. She should be at the headquarters.~
@644  = ~She's right over there.~
@645  = ~Please report this detail to Manip Limmon. You should find her in the headquarters. I am eager to hear about your achievements.~
@646  = ~Please report this detail to Manip Limmon. You should find her in the headquarters. Please come with me now.~
@647  = ~Please report this detail to Manip Limmon. You should find her in the headquarters. Will you come with me now?~
@648  = ~Please report this detail to Manip Limmon. You should find her in the headquarters.~
@649  = ~Hey! I see you meddling with the captain's personal stuff. This is definitely not for you! I'll take this and you'll get out of here *very* quickly! Be glad I'll let you live!~
@650  = ~Oh, what a bad misunderstanding! You see, I am in the middle of an investigation with regard to smuggler activities in the region. We received a hint that your captain fell prey to a dubious vendor. I was here to silently remove the incriminating evidence. See here, I have an official pass and all.~
@651  = ~Oh, what a bad misunderstanding! You see, I am in the middle of an investigation with regard to smuggler activities in the region. We received a hint that your captain fell prey to a dubious vendor. I was here to silently remove the incriminating evidence.~
@652  = ~Listen, I really need this vial of fragrance. Any chance we could settle this with a nice sum of gold?~
@653  = ~All right, all right—I'm off!~
@654  = ~Get out quickly before I decide that you're one of the scoundrels I'm supposed to protect the tower from!~
@655  = ~Smugglers?~
@656  = ~Listen, I know what you did for this tower and this city.~
@657  = ~You can't just come here and take stuff! But you sound honest enough. If this is true, this is a serious matter.~
@658  = ~I'll put this vial somewhere safe, and you go talk to our captain about this, all right? I can't just give it to you, I'm sure you'll understand.~
@659  = ~This is from Amn! Hmm. If this is true, this is a serious matter.~
@660  = ~This is no place for you at this hour! Leave at once.~
@661  = ~What is this? I-I tried *so hard* not to let myself be known but this is just too much! Your colleague told me I should be really, really quiet for everything to go smoothly and yet, here I am!~
@662  = ~Er...~
@663  = ~Who are you?~
@664  = ~You were in there of your own free will?~
@665  = ~You were inside the barrel?~
@666  = ~Fear not! I saved you from inside that barrel, dear sir!~
@667  = ~Are you kidding me?~
@668  = ~Great—just great. I spent a *whole* lot of gold to get out of town undetected, and now it's all for nothing! Here I am now, outside this barrel in gods know where. You will *not* get the second part of the fee, I tell you!~
@669  = ~Excuse me, I thought someone in there was in danger!~
@670  = ~Why did you let yourself be smuggled out of the city?~
@671  = ~So you are a criminal?!~
@672  = ~Wait, wait—I belong to the smugglers. Just get back in there and I'll bring you to the promised contact. You have my word!~
@673  = ~You want to just go now? After the effort you took to be in that barrel?~
@674  = ~Ah, it's not that easy—it never is.~
@675  = ~What? Watch your mouth! Right now, *you* are the criminal here!~
@676  = ~A-aHAHAhaha!~
@677  = ~I guess it doesn't matter to tell you my motive. I am ambassador—ambassador from Crimmor. That is in Amn, in case you are not aware. I... am not fond of being in this city any more. Let's keep the reasons for that private, but I assure you they are not illegal or morally offensive. But to leave officially, I'd need to apply for discharge from my home authorities, there would be farewell ceremonies, I'd need to wait for my official replacement and introduce them appropriately... *sigh* So I bailed. Or, at least, I tried. *scoffs*~
@678  = ~On top of that, current tensions between Baldur's Gate and Amn do not help the overall situation. The city was even sealed until not so long ago. That is why I got into contact with you people in the first place.~
@679  = ~Please understand that I will no longer trust you with my onward journey after you opened the barrel contrary to the explicit agreements.~
@680  = ~Well, I guess this is a sign from the gods that there is no easy way out of some responsibilities. *sigh* Thank you, I'll find my own way from here.~
@681  = ~Ho there! Where's the delivery? Make haste and get it! Tide is good for leaving.~
@682  = ~Ah, here you are. And I see the barrel.~
@683  = ~Let me take this off your hands.~
@684  = ~Your work here is done. Do not follow us!~
@685  = ~Let me take this off your... wait! Soldiers!~
@686  = ~Ah, it's good to stretch my legs again. I wasn't sure it would really work to get away from my duties like this... Who are all these people?~
@687  = ~Don't worry, they are just our muscle. They were leaving.~
@688  = ~Er, yes, I am leaving. Good journey, sir.~
@689  = ~Is Holger into human smuggling now?~
@690  = ~Why... are they posing questions? You guaranteed that I would leave the city completely incognito.~
@691  = ~They are not. They were *leaving*.~
@692  = ~<CHARNAME>! What happened to the planned smuggler bust? We waited, but no one showed! You wasted our time, as it seems!~
@693  = ~No, I thought I was rescuing someone from inside the barrel, I swear! But then the whole operation blew apart.~
@694  = ~I apologize. An important mission led me astray and I didn't have enough time.~
@695  = ~I would have told the captain, but you beat me to it.~
@696  = ~Whoops, yeah, I'm not sure what happened there, either.~
@697  = ~Hmph, it's a failed mission then. You should take this more seriously!~
@698  = ~Hmph, it's a failed mission then. Well, these things can happen. Let us know if you'll hear anything else.~
@699  = ~You are arrested for trafficking and smuggling! The evidence is inside this barrel!~
@700  = ~Ah, it's good to stretch my legs... Wait—soldiers? That's... not going as planned, I see.~
@701  = ~Oh, ambassador! I was informed about your sudden leave, your note raised quite the commotion inside the palace. Were you in any kind of danger, sir?~
@702  = ~What? No-no, no, no, no danger. I just... just wanted to return home. I apologize for the confusion I caused. I guess this is a sign from the gods that there is no easy way out of some responsibilities. *sigh*~
@703  = ~We will accompany you back to the palace if you let us, sir.~
@704  = ~Well, this is not exactly the kind of smuggler wares I was hoping for, but we caught this man and his colleagues red handed. <CHARNAME>, come by the captain's office later to get your reward!~
@705  = ~I serve the Flaming Fist!~
@706  = ~<CHARNAME>! It is good to see you again—and in such good standing, as it is.~
@707  = ~Holger! Are you back at Baldur's Gate now?~
@708  = ~Do you have more smuggler quests for me?~
@709  = ~Sorry, but I do not have time for you.~
@710  = ~I am, indeed.~
@711  = ~I do, indeed.~
@712  = ~I would have one thing I could use your help with. Seeing how you go in and out of the palace nowadays, I could use your assistance... *securing* a collaboration I'm currently forging.~
@713  = ~Oh no, I'm not a man of inefficient little things, <CHARNAME>. Well, yes of *course* I am also interested in the Duke's private stash—but it is by no means my main interest. Plus, with your help, I'll actually secure unlimited access to it, among other things.~
@714  = ~Appreciated. Yes, I was worried about seeing your likeness for a bounty all over the place. But you found your way back into the city—and quite impressive, if I may say so!~
@715  = ~Well, then see you around, I guess.~
@716  = ~Give this to a Flaming Fist guard inside the palace. His name is Bruce. As far as I know, he should be on duty on the second floor. Just give it to him and give him my greetings. He'll know.~
@717  = ~Can I count on you?~
@718  = ~Do you want me to get ruby wine from the palace's cellar, too?~
@719  = ~Of course. What do you need me to do?~
@720  = ~I'm sorry, I can't do that.~
@721  = ~It's always good to see you.~
@722  = ~I heard back from Bruce. Well done, <CHARNAME>. Shuck's "replacement" is installed, now I am fully back in business!~
@723  = ~It's a real shame, <CHARNAME>, but you've become *too* famous to be a good smuggler. Well, at least for the kind of business I do. You should aim for bigger and more political coups if you want to stay in the trade. Again, it was a pleasure working with you, but this is farewell. You won't see me again, not if I'm as good at staying in the shadows as I always claim to be. Farewell.~
@724  = ~[BG:EE #7550]Move along, friends, just make sure not to make any trouble.~
@725  = ~[BG:EE #7549]You there. Make sure you don't make any trouble.~
@726  = ~I have this for you, with greetings from Holger.~
@727  = ~I'm on my way.~
@728  = ~Holger? What the... A-ahahaha! This is *brilliant*. I told him to impress me and, well—having you deliver this to me, *here* of all places—yes, I am impressed. Tell him I'm in—actually, I can tell him myself. It's my break time now. See you around!~
@729  = ~Hey, I have a message for <CHARNAME>. Greetings from Holger. If you are available, please come and meet him in the Three Old Kegs.~
@730  = ~Well, *actually* I am here because we noticed you are in trouble. We won't let one of us stand in the rain, so Holger wants me to say 'hello'.~
@731  = ~Well, I'm here because we noticed your troubles. We won't let one of us stand in the rain, no? I'm a smuggler, at your service, and Holger wants me to say 'hello'.~
@732  = ~Oh, wait a minute. I think I know you from our associate, Holger. Rings a bell? He praised you to the skies for your big help in reinstating smuggling business in Baldur's Gate. Well, in this case I am even more inclined to make you a good offer.~
@733  = ~[PC Reply]How is the smuggler investigation in the Helm and Cloak inn coming along?~
@734  = ~[Manip Limmon]We will bust them soon. Come back later to get your reward.~
